(Severity: High) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Description: Passwords are hashed with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 using only 1,000 iterations. This is) Tj T*
(substantially weaker than modern password-storage recommendations and makes offline cracking) Tj T*
(cheaper if hashes are obtained.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Evidence: lib/password.ts calls pbkdf2 with 1000 iterations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Impact: Compromised password hashes may be cracked more efficiently, especially when users reuse) Tj T*
(passwords.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Recommended fix: Use a modern password hashing algorithm such as Argon2id or a properly) Tj T*
(configured bcrypt cost factor. Store an algorithm/version marker so hashes can be upgraded after) Tj T*
(successful login. Use constant-time comparison and enforce server-side password policy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Verification evidence: Add tests confirming new passwords use the selected algorithm and that) Tj T*
(legacy hashes are migrated safely.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(VULN-04: Hard-Coded JWT Secret Fallback) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Severity: Critical) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Description: The JWT implementation uses your-secret-key-change-in-production when JWT_SECRET is) Tj T*
(missing.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Evidence: lib/auth.ts defines the signing key using process.env.JWT_SECRET) Tj T*
("your-secret-key-change-in-production".) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Impact: If the environment variable is absent, an attacker who knows the source code can forge) Tj T*
(tokens and impersonate any role, including administrators.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Recommended fix: Fail application startup when JWT_SECRET is missing or too short. Use a) Tj T*
(high-entropy deployment secret, rotate secrets safely, and avoid embedding fallback credentials) Tj T*
(in source code.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Verification evidence: Start the application without JWT_SECRET; the secure version should fail) Tj T*
(configuration validation rather than start with a default key.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(VULN-05: Broken Object-Level Authorization / IDOR) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Severity: High) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Description: Doctor-only creation endpoints accept arbitrary patient identifiers. They check the) Tj T*
(caller's role but do not verify that the doctor is authorized to access the selected patient.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Evidence: app/api/medical-records/create/route.ts and the prescription creation route accept) Tj T*
(patientId without checking a valid doctor-patient appointment, hospital relationship, or) Tj T*
(treatment relationship.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Impact: A doctor account could create or manipulate records belonging to unrelated patients.) Tj T*
ET

endstream
